  "relayBlind": {
    "note": "The contract says bootstrap and runtime payloads are relayed blind by the launcher and the worker. These mux negatives are refused by the TypeScript parser (daemon side) for their PAYLOAD and are therefore deliberately PASSED THROUGH by the worker, which checks headers only for those classes. The C test asserts exactly this. Round 3 adds the conversation-set, runtime-ready, bootstrap-fault and runtime-stream negatives: the contract says a runtime payload's 4 byte prefix is read by the endpoints only (the worker relays runtime frames without reading them) and the conversation set's 20,480 byte cap is the guest's own, so every one of them is passed through. The control-up `fault` negatives are the worker's and are refused. Contract 6c1f04e8 adds four conversation-set negatives (unixId 1000-65533, a gid equal to a tool uid): bootstrap payloads, passed through.",
